{"name":"Azure Sky","hex":"#b0e0f6","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"azure","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The clear blue colour of the sky; also, a pigment or dye of this colour.","A blue colour on a coat of arms, represented in engraving by horizontal parallel lines."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Sky blue; resembling the clear blue colour of the unclouded sky.","Cloudless."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/azure"},{"term":"sky","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Sikaiana."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The atmosphere above a given point, especially as visible from the surface of the Earth as the place where the sun, moon, stars, and clouds are seen.","With a descriptive word: the part of the sky which can be seen from a specific place or at a specific time; its climate, condition, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sky"}],"entries":[{"title":"Azure (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Azure is the color between cyan and blue on the color wheel. It is often described as the color of the sky on a clear day."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Azulejos_Parque_Eduardo_VII-2.jpg/330px-Azulejos_Parque_Eduardo_VII-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azure_(color)","wikidata":"Q373058"},{"title":"Sky","blocks":[{"text":"The sky is an unobstructed view upward from the surface of the Earth. It includes the atmosphere and outer space. It may also be considered a place between the ground and outer space, thus distinct from outer space."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg/330px-July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky","wikidata":"Q527"},{"title":"Sky blue","blocks":[{"text":"Sky blue refers to a collection of shades comparable to that of a clear daytime sky. Typically it is a shade of cyan or light teal, though some iterations are closer to light azure or light blue. The term is attested from 1681. A 1585 translation of Nicolas de Nicolay's 1576 Les navigations, peregrinations et voyages faicts en la Turquie includes \"the tulbant [turban] of the merchant must be skie coloured\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Shades_of_light_blue.png/330px-Shades_of_light_blue.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky_blue","wikidata":"Q373160"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/ChalcanthitefranceII.jpg/500px-ChalcanthitefranceII.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/ChalcanthitefranceII.jpg/1280px-ChalcanthitefranceII.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/ChalcanthitefranceII.jpg/500px-ChalcanthitefranceII.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChalcanthitefranceII.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Alcedo_azurea_-_Julatten_%28cropped%29_2_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Alcedo_azurea_-_Julatten_%28cropped%29_2_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Alcedo_azurea_-_Julatten_%28cropped%29_2_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Alcedo_azurea_-_Julatten_%28cropped%29_2_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Alcedo_azurea_-_Julatten_%28cropped%29_2_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Alcedo